### What is the default deserializer in Kafka?

Kafka itself is type-agnostic and moves bytes. The raw client's default is `ByteArrayDeserializer`, and a typical Spring Boot setup defaults to `StringDeserializer`, giving you JSON as a plain string that you parse yourself.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class StringDeserializerListener {
    // With StringDeserializer, the value is a raw JSON string
    @KafkaListener(topics = "settlements", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void onSettlement(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record) {
        String jsonString = record.value();
        System.out.println("Raw JSON: " + jsonString);
        // You must parse this yourself with ObjectMapper
    }
}
```

```text
Raw JSON: {"txnId":"TXN-1","accountId":"SAV2001","amount":50000}
```

### How do you configure JsonDeserializer for typed consumption?

To get a strongly typed object directly, set the value deserializer to Spring Kafka's `JsonDeserializer`. It uses Jackson under the hood to reconstruct the payload into your target type.

```properties
# application.properties
spring.kafka.consumer.value-deserializer=org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.JsonDeserializer
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.json.value.default.type=com.bank.model.TxnEvent
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.json.trusted.packages=com.bank.model
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TxnEvent {
    private String txnId;
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal amount;

    // getters, setters, toString
    public String getTxnId() { return txnId; }
    public void setTxnId(String txnId) { this.txnId = txnId; }
    public String getAccountId() { return accountId; }
    public void setAccountId(String accountId) { this.accountId = accountId; }
    public BigDecimal getAmount() { return amount; }
    public void setAmount(BigDecimal amount) { this.amount = amount; }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "TxnEvent{txnId=" + txnId + ", account=" + accountId + ", amount=" + amount + "}";
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import com.bank.model.TxnEvent;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class TypedJsonListener {
    @KafkaListener(topics = "settlements", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void onSettlement(TxnEvent event) {
        // Jackson already deserialized the JSON into TxnEvent
        System.out.println("Typed event: " + event);
        System.out.println("Account: " + event.getAccountId()
            + " Amount: " + event.getAmount());
    }
}
```

```text
Typed event: TxnEvent{txnId=TXN-1, account=SAV2001, amount=50000}
Account: SAV2001 Amount: 50000
```

### Why are trusted packages important for security?

By default `JsonDeserializer` refuses to instantiate arbitrary classes named in a type header. A malicious producer could stamp a dangerous type header and trick the deserializer into building an unexpected class. Trusted packages is an allowlist. Setting it to `*` trusts everything and is a security risk in production.

```properties
# CORRECT: only trust your own model package
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.json.trusted.packages=com.bank.model

# WRONG: trusts everything -- security risk in production
# spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.json.trusted.packages=*
```

### How do you wrap JsonDeserializer with ErrorHandlingDeserializer?

If a JSON payload is malformed, the raw JsonDeserializer throws on the poll thread, blocking the partition in an infinite retry loop (the poison pill). `ErrorHandlingDeserializer` catches the exception and converts it into a recoverable error that flows to the error handler.

```properties
# application.properties -- wrap with ErrorHandlingDeserializer
spring.kafka.consumer.value-deserializer=org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.ErrorHandlingDeserializer
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.deserializer.value.delegate.class=org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.JsonDeserializer
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.json.value.default.type=com.bank.model.TxnEvent
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.json.trusted.packages=com.bank.model
```

```text
# Malformed JSON payload arrives:
# {"txnId":"TXN-1","accountId":"SAV2001","amount":INVALID}
# Without ErrorHandlingDeserializer: partition stuck forever
# With ErrorHandlingDeserializer: error handler routes to DLT, partition continues
```

### How do you pause a listener at runtime?

Every @KafkaListener can have an id. You inject `KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry`, look up the container by id, and call `pause()`. The consumer stays alive, holds its partitions and heartbeats (no rebalance), but stops delivering records.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.config.KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry;
import org.springframework.kafka.listener.MessageListenerContainer;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ListenerFlowControl {
    private final KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry registry;
    private final DatabaseHealthChecker dbHealth;

    public ListenerFlowControl(KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry registry,
                               DatabaseHealthChecker dbHealth) {
        this.registry = registry;
        this.dbHealth = dbHealth;
    }

    @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 5000)
    public void checkAndControl() {
        MessageListenerContainer container = registry.getListenerContainer("settlement");
        if (container == null) return;

        if (!dbHealth.isHealthy() && container.isRunning() && !container.isContainerPaused()) {
            container.pause();
            System.out.println("Paused settlement listener -- DB is down");
        } else if (dbHealth.isHealthy() && container.isContainerPaused()) {
            container.resume();
            System.out.println("Resumed settlement listener -- DB recovered");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Paused settlement listener -- DB is down
Resumed settlement listener -- DB recovered
```

### Why is pausing better than letting the listener keep failing?

Continued failures would burn through retries, flood the dead letter topic with transient errors, or risk the consumer being kicked out of the group for exceeding `max.poll.interval.ms`. Pausing cleanly holds position without any of these costs.

```text
# Without pause (DB down):
# Record 1 -> process -> DB error -> retry 1, 2, 3 -> DLT (transient error in DLT!)
# Record 2 -> process -> DB error -> retry 1, 2, 3 -> DLT
# DLT fills with records that would have succeeded if DB was up
# Consumer may exceed max.poll.interval.ms and get kicked

# With pause (DB down):
# Pause listener -> consumer stays in group, no records delivered
# DB recovers -> resume -> pick up from last committed offset
# Zero records lost, zero DLT pollution
```

### How does the listener method receive a typed object with a listener id?

Combining JSON deserialization with a listener id for pause/resume control:

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import com.bank.model.TxnEvent;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.kafka.support.Acknowledgment;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class SettlementJsonListener {

    @KafkaListener(id = "settlement", topics = "settlements", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void onSettlement(TxnEvent event, Acknowledgment ack) {
        System.out.println("Settlement: " + event.getAccountId()
            + " amount=" + event.getAmount());
        settlementService.process(event);
        ack.acknowledge();
    }
}
```

```text
Settlement: SAV2001 amount=50000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Pausing takes effect after the current poll's records are processed. Drive pause/resume from a health indicator or circuit breaker, not a manual flag. The ErrorHandlingDeserializer is non-negotiable in production: without it, one corrupt record can stall an entire partition. And never use `trusted.packages=*` in production -- always name your own packages explicitly.

```properties
# Production-grade deserializer chain
spring.kafka.consumer.value-deserializer=org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.ErrorHandlingDeserializer
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.deserializer.value.delegate.class=org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.JsonDeserializer
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.json.value.default.type=com.bank.model.TxnEvent
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.spring.json.trusted.packages=com.bank.model
```
